1. Skewness
What is Skewness?

Skewness measures how asymmetric a distribution is.

A perfectly symmetric distribution means the left side and right side are
mirror images.
Example: Gaussian (Normal) distribution (skewness=0)
symmetric distribution:
-Gaussian distribution is a symmetric distribution.
-we have a two types of skewness distribution

1.right skewed 
if the deviation is more on right side then it is a right 
skewed distribution (skewness > 0)+ve skewed
mean > median > mode


2.Left skewed (skewness < 0)
if the deviation is more on the left side then it is a left
skewed distribution .
mean < median < mode

-skewness is defining how far the particular distribution
is from the symmetric
 skewness=0 ---->Normal distribution 
 skewness>0----->right skewed distribution 
 skewness<0----->left skewed distribution .
 formula:

 Skewness=n/(n−1)(n−2)​∑((xi​−xˉ/s)​3)
n	number of observations
xi	each data point
x̄	mean
s	standard deviation
 skewness(data)=(n/(n-1)*(n-2))*(summ(((x-x-bar)/sd)**3)) 

